Let $X$ be a standard Borel space and let $\vec{G} = (X, E)$ be a directed graph on $X$ whose edge set $E \subseteq X \times X$ is Borel. Assume:

(1) $\vec{G}$ is locally finite, i.e., for every vertex $x \in X$ both the in-neighborhood $\{y : (y,x) \in E\}$ and the out-neighborhood $N^+(x) = \{y : (x,y) \in E\}$ are finite;

(2) the in-degrees are uniformly bounded, i.e., there exists $d \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $|\{y : (y,x) \in E\}| \le d$ for every $x \in X$;

(3) the out-neighborhoods have polynomial growth, i.e., there exist constants $C > 0$ and $k \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for every $x \in X$ and every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, the number $|B_n^+(x)|$ of vertices reachable from $x$ by a directed path of length at most $n$ satisfies $|B_n^+(x)| \le C n^k$.

Let $E_{\vec{G}}$ be the equivalence relation on $X$ defined by $x \, E_{\vec{G}} \, y$ iff $x$ and $y$ are in the same connected component of the underlying undirected graph of $\vec{G}$. Recall that a countable Borel equivalence relation $F$ on a standard Borel space is hyperfinite if it is the union of an increasing sequence $F_0 \subseteq F_1 \subseteq \cdots$ of Borel equivalence relations all of whose classes are finite.

Question: Must $E_{\vec{G}}$ be hyperfinite?

